00:23:14.960 --> 00:23:16.143
it should be unjudged.

00:23:18.060 --> 00:23:20.720
But there is also sort of the lottery of--

00:23:20.720 --> 00:23:22.360
Why is Jade living so long?

00:23:22.360 --> 00:23:24.290
Well she's--
She's 75.

00:23:24.290 --> 00:23:25.123
She's amazing.

00:23:26.020 --> 00:23:27.700
I've been telling you.

00:23:27.700 --> 00:23:29.880
But I do like the idea,
first of all, okay--

00:23:29.880 --> 00:23:31.290
Why is she a puppy again?

00:23:31.290 --> 00:23:33.670
Maybe here's a reason not to clone the dog

00:23:33.670 --> 00:23:36.420
from an ethical standpoint,
because dogs need

00:23:36.420 --> 00:23:39.829
to be adopted, right, so by cloning a dog,

00:23:39.829 --> 00:23:42.100
you're bringing another dog into the world

00:23:42.100 --> 00:23:43.770
as opposed to adopting another dog,

00:23:43.770 --> 00:23:47.040
so I could adopt another dog
that needs a home next time

00:23:47.040 --> 00:23:48.140
just like we did with Barbara.

00:23:48.140 --> 00:23:50.050
Yeah and I think--

00:23:50.050 --> 00:23:51.420
So there's a reason to not do it.

00:23:51.420 --> 00:23:54.170
There's something to
pair with that which is

00:23:54.170 --> 00:23:55.690
it is a relationship.

00:23:55.690 --> 00:23:59.280
I mean, our dogs are family members.

00:23:59.280 --> 00:24:03.060
They're dog family members
but they are family members

00:24:03.060 --> 00:24:06.350
so it's a relationship that then,

00:24:06.350 --> 00:24:09.120
you just don't wanna have,
you don't wanna fabricate

00:24:09.120 --> 00:24:14.120
just for your own convenience
a relationship with a clone.

00:24:14.180 --> 00:24:17.400
It just seems like you're just trying

00:24:17.400 --> 00:24:22.400
to perpetuate something and
deny something that is a part

00:24:23.010 --> 00:24:24.630
of life which is death,

00:24:24.630 --> 00:24:27.705
and an opportunity to
forge a new relationship

00:24:27.705 --> 00:24:31.693
with all of its challenges and joys.

00:24:36.098 --> 00:24:37.880
Yeah, what if the new dog's a?

00:24:37.880 --> 00:24:38.793
Figuratively?

00:24:39.700 --> 00:24:41.003
Then--
Yeah.

00:24:42.165 --> 00:24:44.735
Yeah that's the risk.

00:24:44.735 --> 00:24:46.810
So we got somebody working
on our house right now,

00:24:46.810 --> 00:24:50.590
the contractor who, of
course, Barbara loves

00:24:50.590 --> 00:24:51.873
because he is a person.

00:24:53.130 --> 00:24:56.520
And the way he described
her the other day,

00:24:56.520 --> 00:24:58.280
this may be weird to have a relationship

00:24:58.280 --> 00:24:59.360
with your contractor in this way

00:24:59.360 --> 00:25:00.360
but he was talking about Barbara

00:25:00.360 --> 00:25:02.087
and he was like she is pure love.

00:25:02.087 --> 00:25:03.930
She is pure love.
She's is pure love.

00:25:03.930 --> 00:25:06.270
That's what Barbara is,
Barbara is just like, again--

00:25:06.270 --> 00:25:08.570
You're not gonna get more pure love out

00:25:08.570 --> 00:25:13.160
of another dog so that's, the risk is,

00:25:13.160 --> 00:25:17.030
it's a guarantee that with your next dog,

00:25:17.030 --> 00:25:19.000
you won't get that much love.

00:25:19.000 --> 00:25:23.440
Right and so I mean I'm just like okay,

00:25:23.440 --> 00:25:25.760
well this is such a good thing.

00:25:25.760 --> 00:25:27.310
We got such a good thing going.

00:25:29.400 --> 00:25:31.067
Am I willing to take people's--

00:25:32.717 --> 00:25:35.720
It feels weird--
Sideways glances at me.

00:25:35.720 --> 00:25:37.810
It's not like she's
gonna wear a doggy shirt

00:25:37.810 --> 00:25:40.573
that says I'm a clone, not the original.

00:25:42.050 --> 00:25:43.400
That would be funny though.

00:25:45.120 --> 00:25:48.310
I mean what about cloning--

00:25:48.310 --> 00:25:49.143
People?

00:25:49.143 --> 00:25:53.340
Yeah let's clone our wives
if they pass untimely.

00:25:53.340 --> 00:25:55.290
I mean I think that's why it's so weird,

00:25:55.290 --> 00:25:57.290
it's like you'd never do that.

00:25:57.290 --> 00:25:59.570
You'd never do that to a human.

00:25:59.570 --> 00:26:00.623
It's unnatural.

00:26:02.133 --> 00:26:04.930
There's other reasons why
we wouldn't clone our wives

00:26:04.930 --> 00:26:07.960
because they would be a baby.

00:26:07.960 --> 00:26:10.853
Yeah, you gotta, well
that's what I was trying

00:26:10.853 --> 00:26:11.820
to tell you about--
It's like this is gonna

00:26:11.820 --> 00:26:13.850
be awkward for quite some time.

00:26:13.850 --> 00:26:15.540
That's why I was trying
to tell you about Twilight

00:26:15.540 --> 00:26:17.320
that I didn't get to.

00:26:17.320 --> 00:26:22.320
Was that that wolf guy, he
made a connection with the baby

00:26:23.240 --> 00:26:26.320
and then waited for it to
grow old and then they became,

00:26:26.320 --> 00:26:27.380
they hooked up.

00:26:27.380 --> 00:26:28.690
That's creepy.

00:26:28.690 --> 00:26:33.690
With the vampire's girl's baby.

00:26:34.180 --> 00:26:36.208
Don't think of it in the
context of a relationship

00:26:36.208 --> 00:26:37.041
because that's--
I'm telling you,

00:26:37.041 --> 00:26:38.060
there's so much in that Twilight.

00:26:38.060 --> 00:26:39.490
I could go back to it.

00:26:39.490 --> 00:26:40.610
That's creepy.

00:26:40.610 --> 00:26:42.250
But let's just say there's, okay,

00:26:42.250 --> 00:26:44.670
there's a great mind,

00:26:44.670 --> 00:26:47.770
like a person who does
incredible things, right.

00:26:47.770 --> 00:26:50.330
I don't know, name someone who we consider

00:26:50.330 --> 00:26:54.540
to be incredible that
does incredible things.

00:26:54.540 --> 00:26:56.670
That Ryan's ToysReview.

00:26:56.670 --> 00:27:00.470
Okay exactly, somebody who
has just impacted culture

00:27:00.470 --> 00:27:02.660
for the better and we're
like well when Ryan

00:27:02.660 --> 00:27:04.570
from Ryan's ToyReview dies.

00:27:06.090 --> 00:27:07.827
What are we gonna do, we need him.

00:27:08.793 --> 00:27:10.730
We could get, and when he's a baby,

00:27:10.730 --> 00:27:12.090
it's like you don't have
to wait long for him

00:27:12.090 --> 00:27:13.150
to do his thing again.

00:27:13.150 --> 00:27:15.350
That's right, like within
three years basically.

00:27:15.350 --> 00:27:17.840
Instant gratification almost
from a clone perspective.

00:27:17.840 --> 00:27:20.260
The channel would be dark
for just a couple years.

00:27:20.260 --> 00:27:21.610
Right.

00:27:21.610 --> 00:27:23.050
For that time you could just do videos

00:27:23.050 --> 00:27:24.880
that are just like retrospective,

00:27:24.880 --> 00:27:26.780
like this is what you're
gonna get, don't forget.

00:27:26.780 --> 00:27:29.670
Toddler toys, they
could start much earlier

00:27:29.670 --> 00:27:30.650
than they actually did.
Start him as a baby.

00:27:30.650 --> 00:27:32.670
Put him as just a newborn.

00:27:32.670 --> 00:27:34.470
Newborn toys.

00:27:34.470 --> 00:27:38.620
Are there channels where
parents just get their newborns

00:27:38.620 --> 00:27:41.250
to review products?

00:27:41.250 --> 00:27:43.952
Just throw a rattle in the
cage and see what happens?

00:27:45.800 --> 00:27:46.950
Well there should be.

00:27:47.830 --> 00:27:48.710
There should be.

00:27:48.710 --> 00:27:50.493
I mean I think you can exploit your child

00:27:50.493 --> 00:27:53.712
from a very early age if you want to.

00:27:53.712 --> 00:27:55.029
Did I answer your question?

00:27:55.029 --> 00:27:56.823
I don't think you have to
wait until they're five.

00:27:58.360 --> 00:28:01.140
With our Ryan's ToyReview
thought experiment,

00:28:01.140 --> 00:28:02.390
did we reach a conclusion?

00:28:02.390 --> 00:28:04.560
Well first of all, can
human cloning be done?

00:28:04.560 --> 00:28:06.730
I don't think human cloning
can be done as effectively

00:28:06.730 --> 00:28:10.810
and reliably, right, or is it
just unethical at this point?

00:28:10.810 --> 00:28:14.800
But could they really do it
and they wouldn't be screwed up

00:28:14.800 --> 00:28:16.080
in some weird way?

00:28:16.080 --> 00:28:18.423
Where is the science
of cloning these days?

00:28:19.410 --> 00:28:23.815
There was the experiment,
I think it was in China

00:28:23.815 --> 00:28:28.815
a few months back where they
were doing genetic mutations

00:28:29.370 --> 00:28:33.870
for medical purposes but
it wasn't creating a clone.

00:28:33.870 --> 00:28:35.610
It was using CRISPR on people.

00:28:35.610 --> 00:28:37.390
But then there were--
Oh yeah gene edited kids,

00:28:37.390 --> 00:28:38.290
yeah.
Gene editing kids

00:28:38.290 --> 00:28:40.240
and there wasn't--
That's happening.

00:28:41.640 --> 00:28:44.413
There's side effects.

00:28:45.290 --> 00:28:49.530
There's genetic side effects.

00:28:49.530 --> 00:28:53.480
You try to get rid of this one thing,

00:28:53.480 --> 00:28:55.500
but then other things pop up.

00:28:55.500 --> 00:29:00.500
Well okay so what if they
could create a clone for you

00:29:01.680 --> 00:29:04.180
that didn't have a head?

00:29:04.180 --> 00:29:05.170
Of a dog?

00:29:05.170 --> 00:29:06.790
No of yourself.
Okay.

00:29:06.790 --> 00:29:08.537
And they were like okay, we're
growing this clone of you

00:29:08.537 --> 00:29:10.160
that doesn't have a head

00:29:10.160 --> 00:29:12.793
and we're gonna harvest
the organs as needed.

00:29:13.630 --> 00:29:16.240
You need a heart, oh we
got your heart over here.

00:29:16.240 --> 00:29:18.250
That's cool.
In this baby.

00:29:18.250 --> 00:29:20.900
In this baby without a head,
you're gonna get a baby heart.

00:29:20.900 --> 00:29:22.720
You have to wait for the
baby to get old enough.

00:29:22.720 --> 00:29:24.520
The headless baby's gotta grow up,

00:29:25.660 --> 00:29:26.830
gotta review some rattles.

00:29:26.830 --> 00:29:29.510
I haven't really spent
a lot of time thinking

00:29:29.510 --> 00:29:32.440
about the ethics of this
but it does sound like

00:29:32.440 --> 00:29:34.570
a can of worms that we
probably don't wanna open.

00:29:34.570 --> 00:29:36.370
We don't need more people,
we've got too many.

00:29:36.370 --> 00:29:37.203
We don't need to clone the ones we have.

00:29:37.203 --> 00:29:39.514
All worms are cloned, by the way.

00:29:39.514 --> 00:29:41.436
That is true, isn't it?

00:29:41.436 --> 00:29:43.150
Are they asexual?

00:29:43.150 --> 00:29:44.400
They all look the same.

00:29:45.350 --> 00:29:48.751
Worms are not asexual,
I'm sure some worms are.

00:29:48.751 --> 00:29:51.190
Oh, I was messin' with you.

00:29:51.190 --> 00:29:52.733
Let's ask this next question.

00:29:55.180 --> 00:29:58.760
Jennifer Hadorn asks how do
you know when a new place

00:29:58.760 --> 00:30:02.300
you move to finally gets
that feeling of home?

00:30:02.300 --> 00:30:03.570
In quotes.

00:30:03.570 --> 00:30:05.720
What are the types of
things you experience

00:30:05.720 --> 00:30:07.669
that make it a home?

00:30:07.669 --> 00:30:10.100
You both had to move
far from North Carolina

00:30:10.100 --> 00:30:12.450
which you have many fond memories of

00:30:12.450 --> 00:30:15.130
but do you still consider
it your one true home

00:30:15.130 --> 00:30:18.330
or do you get the same
feelings about California now

00:30:18.330 --> 00:30:22.850
as you did with North Carolina?

00:30:22.850 --> 00:30:24.260
Just as a side note,

00:30:24.260 --> 00:30:29.260
I find myself watching our
Mythical road trip videos.

00:30:30.780 --> 00:30:32.460
From Fuquay to LA.

00:30:32.460 --> 00:30:34.633
This is Rhett and Link's America.

00:30:37.880 --> 00:30:39.320
And so it begins.

00:30:39.320 --> 00:30:42.690
The Mythical road trip
with Rhett and Link.

00:30:42.690 --> 00:30:46.100
I can't remember exactly,
I think in preparation

00:30:46.100 --> 00:30:51.100
for VidCon and a potential
bit about being on YouTube

00:30:51.810 --> 00:30:53.890
for over a decade

00:30:53.890 --> 00:30:56.050
'cause that's how long
VidCon's been going on too.

00:30:56.050 --> 00:30:57.500
10 years, it's the 10th year.

00:30:59.610 --> 00:31:02.310
I ended up going on a rabbit
trail of our own videos

00:31:02.310 --> 00:31:04.080
after looking at other people's,

00:31:04.080 --> 00:31:05.940
but I watched our, if you don't know,

00:31:05.940 --> 00:31:08.963
our Mythical road trips,
when we moved out to LA,

00:31:10.168 --> 00:31:14.770
we had fan meetups at rest
stops all across America

00:31:14.770 --> 00:31:17.390
and we documented the whole
thing and we made all types

00:31:17.390 --> 00:31:19.130
of videos, some on the
Rhett and Link channel,

00:31:19.130 --> 00:31:21.360
some on what ultimately became

00:31:21.360 --> 00:31:22.510
the Good Mythical Morning channel,

00:31:22.510 --> 00:31:24.910
it was just our second
vlog channel at the time.

00:31:25.950 --> 00:31:28.550
But we captured conversations
about our expectations

00:31:30.017 --> 00:31:33.870
of moving to LA and our hopes
and fears were kinda peppered

00:31:33.870 --> 00:31:36.970
into the story of meeting Mythical Beasts

00:31:36.970 --> 00:31:38.840
and seeing parts of the country,

00:31:38.840 --> 00:31:40.770
having never driven across the country.

00:31:40.770 --> 00:31:43.021
Mm-hmm.
It was a sweet time

00:31:43.021 --> 00:31:44.610
in our friendship, Rhett.

00:31:44.610 --> 00:31:47.000
I'm glad we documented that.

00:31:47.000 --> 00:31:49.570
And some people commented,
I'm glad I found this,

00:31:49.570 --> 00:31:51.180
it's edited really well.

00:31:51.180 --> 00:31:52.013
We didn't edit it.

00:31:52.013 --> 00:31:53.290
Chris McCaleb did.

00:31:53.290 --> 00:31:55.285
Yeah shout out to him but anyway,

00:31:55.285 --> 00:31:58.700
it brought back memories
of that moment when we're,

00:31:58.700 --> 00:32:01.259
yeah, it was a big deal
to finally uproot both

00:32:01.259 --> 00:32:05.840
of our families, throw
all of our possessions,

00:32:05.840 --> 00:32:09.600
actually we threw all the
possessions that we needed

00:32:09.600 --> 00:32:12.970
to move into furnished
apartments so we shared a U-Haul

00:32:12.970 --> 00:32:17.970
and then we towed my minivan
and then you and I drove out

00:32:18.160 --> 00:32:23.160
and our families flew out
once we got to Los Angeles.

00:32:23.560 --> 00:32:27.860
But I mean, that was a huge milestone

00:32:27.860 --> 00:32:31.603
and yeah, as we did discuss
in a number of those videos,

00:32:32.647 --> 00:32:34.180
we just didn't know.

00:32:34.180 --> 00:32:36.040
We were coming out to
make Commercial Kings

00:32:36.040 --> 00:32:41.040
for IFC, not knowing if it would last more

00:32:41.090 --> 00:32:45.660
than a season and it didn't.

00:32:45.660 --> 00:32:48.670
Anyway, if you wanna watch those, you can.

00:32:48.670 --> 00:32:51.357
They're still out there,
the Mythical road trip but--

00:32:52.420 --> 00:32:57.090
I know that I was probably
more than all four

00:32:57.090 --> 00:32:59.840
of the four of us, probably
more than anyone else,

00:32:59.840 --> 00:33:03.460
I was of a mind that we're not going back.

00:33:03.460 --> 00:33:04.660
I was like we're going out there,

00:33:04.660 --> 00:33:07.670
I don't care how this goes, we're staying.

00:33:07.670 --> 00:33:09.810
You know what I'm saying, my mentality was

00:33:11.550 --> 00:33:12.790
we're gonna go out there and even

00:33:12.790 --> 00:33:17.790
if Commercial Kings doesn't
work, we're gonna do something.

00:33:17.900 --> 00:33:19.630
We need to make it happen out there.

00:33:19.630 --> 00:33:23.809
I was just kinda committed
to it and I think Jessie was

00:33:23.809 --> 00:33:27.463
in a different way
committed to that happening,

00:33:28.750 --> 00:33:32.770
not a this is where your
career is gonna take off,

00:33:32.770 --> 00:33:35.100
but more I'm just ready for something new,

00:33:35.100 --> 00:33:37.000
ready for a change.

00:33:37.000 --> 00:33:39.930
She had grown up, we lived in the house

00:33:41.240 --> 00:33:42.880
that was across the street from the house

00:33:42.880 --> 00:33:45.300
that she came home from the hospital in.

00:33:45.300 --> 00:33:46.740
Really?
Her parents' house was

00:33:46.740 --> 00:33:48.740
across the street from our house

00:33:48.740 --> 00:33:50.900
and she went from living with her parents

00:33:50.900 --> 00:33:53.790
to being in college for
just a couple of years

00:33:53.790 --> 00:33:56.800
until we got married and
we were in Chapel Hill

00:33:56.800 --> 00:33:58.670
but then quickly got back down to Fuquay

00:33:58.670 --> 00:34:01.470
so I think that she was
just ready for a change,

00:34:01.470 --> 00:34:04.520
that's her personality so, but--

00:34:04.520 --> 00:34:06.800
I think there was a bit more trepidation

00:34:06.800 --> 00:34:09.500
on the Neal side associated
with the unknowns

00:34:09.500 --> 00:34:14.500
of uprooting our entire family,

00:34:15.010 --> 00:34:16.083
moving 'em out there.

00:34:17.200 --> 00:34:19.550
Lando was in a crib.

00:34:19.550 --> 00:34:21.010
Yeah Shepherd was two,
I was talking to him

00:34:21.010 --> 00:34:23.710
about this the other day, I
was like you don't really,

00:34:24.630 --> 00:34:26.770
your whole life has been in California.

00:34:26.770 --> 00:34:27.603
Yeah Lando doesn't--

00:34:27.603 --> 00:34:29.790
And even more so with
Lando who was a year.

00:34:29.790 --> 00:34:33.680
He remembers, maybe nothing.

00:34:33.680 --> 00:34:34.990
I don't think Lando remember anything

00:34:34.990 --> 00:34:36.630
because Shepherd was two.

00:34:36.630 --> 00:34:38.350
And he doesn't remember anything.

00:34:38.350 --> 00:34:39.820
No.

00:34:39.820 --> 00:34:41.320
Very, very sketchy stuff but--

00:34:41.320 --> 00:34:42.720
So the risk was low with them.

00:34:42.720 --> 00:34:45.460
But they would go back, we would go back.

00:34:45.460 --> 00:34:46.520
We were out here for six months,

00:34:46.520 --> 00:34:48.700
then we went back for a few weeks

00:34:48.700 --> 00:34:49.770
before we came back out again.

00:34:49.770 --> 00:34:51.320
To get the rest of our stuff.

00:34:52.670 --> 00:34:55.000
I would say that where it stands now,

00:34:55.000 --> 00:34:56.670
we moved in 2011, it's 2019,

00:34:56.670 --> 00:34:58.760
so we just passed our eighth year,

00:34:58.760 --> 00:35:00.873
so we're in our ninth year of being here.

00:35:03.800 --> 00:35:07.533
And interestingly, when
you listen to this,

00:35:08.430 --> 00:35:10.863
we'll be about to go back.

00:35:12.270 --> 00:35:15.140
We're gonna go back to Buies
Creek to create something

00:35:16.440 --> 00:35:18.090
that you'll find out about later.

00:35:18.090 --> 00:35:19.560
But we'll spend some--
Oh snap.

00:35:19.560 --> 00:35:23.333
We'll spend some time, it's
gonna be related to the book.

00:35:24.370 --> 00:35:26.190
Bleak Creek, because Bleak Creek's based

00:35:26.190 --> 00:35:28.720
on Buies Creek loosely.

00:35:28.720 --> 00:35:30.630
More tightly in some ways than others

00:35:30.630 --> 00:35:34.350
but we're gonna go back not
just to where we last lived

00:35:34.350 --> 00:35:35.850
but to our home, where we grew up

00:35:35.850 --> 00:35:39.810
and whenever I get to North Carolina,

00:35:39.810 --> 00:35:41.690
especially when you go during the summer

00:35:41.690 --> 00:35:43.490
and you're immediately
hit with this blanket

00:35:43.490 --> 00:35:45.283
of damp, muggy air.

00:35:46.570 --> 00:35:49.952
I don't think I'll ever lose the sensation

00:35:49.952 --> 00:35:54.952
that I am home, that I'm
really, really, this is home.

00:35:55.280 --> 00:35:57.610
Yeah.
This is where I grew up.

00:35:57.610 --> 00:35:59.890
This is where I became a man.

00:35:59.890 --> 00:36:02.749
The roots, man, you tap into the roots.

00:36:02.749 --> 00:36:06.293
It's like gettin' back on a bicycle.

00:36:06.293 --> 00:36:08.850
It just, it feels right.

00:36:08.850 --> 00:36:11.235
And I don't think there'll ever be a time

00:36:11.235 --> 00:36:14.952
where I'm driving around
Los Angeles or hiking

00:36:14.952 --> 00:36:19.952
in one of these very deserty
landscapes around town

00:36:20.230 --> 00:36:24.240
when you go into nature where I don't feel

00:36:24.240 --> 00:36:26.013
a little bit like a visitor.

00:36:26.950 --> 00:36:28.800
And I just don't think
I'll ever get over that

00:36:28.800 --> 00:36:32.040
because if you moved around
a bunch of different places

00:36:32.040 --> 00:36:33.010
growing up, which we didn't,

00:36:33.010 --> 00:36:38.010
we were basically in Buies Creek
for our entire adolescence,

00:36:40.760 --> 00:36:42.620
I don't think you can lose that,

00:36:42.620 --> 00:36:45.510
so in my deepest core,

00:36:45.510 --> 00:36:47.967
I definitely still consider
North Carolina home,

00:36:47.967 --> 00:36:49.900
and I still feel like a visitor here

00:36:49.900 --> 00:36:52.830
and I don't think that
I can overcome that,

00:36:52.830 --> 00:36:57.830
but I have different feelings
just in a general way.

00:36:58.900 --> 00:37:02.920
I can get into that after
we hear your perspective.

00:37:02.920 --> 00:37:07.920
Well I agree and 100%
relate to tapping back

00:37:08.200 --> 00:37:10.840
into my roots when I go back home,

00:37:10.840 --> 00:37:14.285
like I just said, it is going back home.

00:37:14.285 --> 00:37:17.733
This is where I'm from, this
is where my people still are.

00:37:18.580 --> 00:37:23.513
Family, old friends, landmarks.

00:37:25.620 --> 00:37:28.053
You leave the airport
and you drive back home,

00:37:29.483 --> 00:37:32.800
I got grandparents still
living in the same place

00:37:32.800 --> 00:37:34.300
that they've always been in.

00:37:34.300 --> 00:37:38.760
I got Mom in the same county that

00:37:40.931 --> 00:37:43.320
when I grew up, same place, you know,

00:37:43.320 --> 00:37:45.430
so it's like you travel those same roads

00:37:45.430 --> 00:37:47.080
and you access all of those memories

00:37:47.080 --> 00:37:49.364
and nothing's gonna ever take that away

00:37:49.364 --> 00:37:51.643
and nothing's ever gonna replace that,

00:37:52.620 --> 00:37:57.620
but I do consider in
a different inflection

00:37:58.400 --> 00:38:02.340
of the word home, maybe a
slightly different definition,

00:38:02.340 --> 00:38:07.340
but I do have a feeling that
Los Angeles is my home now.

00:38:10.330 --> 00:38:14.696
It is legitimately home for
me and I think the question is

00:38:14.696 --> 00:38:17.390
how long did it take for
it to get to that point.

00:38:17.390 --> 00:38:19.220
I think a lot of it has to do with

00:38:20.260 --> 00:38:24.690
my kids absolutely
consider this place home.

00:38:24.690 --> 00:38:27.350
This is the place where they've built

00:38:27.350 --> 00:38:32.350
all of those memories and
Lincoln is hard-pressed

00:38:32.779 --> 00:38:37.779
at 14 to have a ton of memories
back in North Carolina.

00:38:39.740 --> 00:38:44.440
Lando has none and so it was
Lily who had to make more

00:38:44.440 --> 00:38:48.310
of a transition but she
was still young enough

00:38:48.310 --> 00:38:51.294
that it wasn't that big of a deal.

00:38:51.294 --> 00:38:55.180
She had a best friend that
she's still in contact with,

00:38:55.180 --> 00:38:58.310
she has a best North
Carolina friend Lizzie

00:38:58.310 --> 00:39:00.550
who was in the Pimp My Stroller video.

00:39:00.550 --> 00:39:02.807
Yeah, right, right.

00:39:04.068 --> 00:39:06.593
She probably had the funniest voiceover.

00:39:07.810 --> 00:39:10.930
Who's to say, there
were so many voiceovers.

00:39:10.930 --> 00:39:15.800
But anyway, I think our
kids considering this home

00:39:15.800 --> 00:39:17.890
goes a long way towards
me considering it home

00:39:17.890 --> 00:39:21.410
'cause we're making our lives here

00:39:21.410 --> 00:39:25.160
and every day is more memories

00:39:25.160 --> 00:39:28.170
that you're putting your roots down.

00:39:28.170 --> 00:39:31.020
But I also think it has to do
with settling in physically

00:39:31.020 --> 00:39:36.020
to a house that, when we first moved out,

00:39:36.060 --> 00:39:39.100
that first six months we
were living in Los Feliz,

00:39:39.100 --> 00:39:43.963
which is super cool in
my opinion, neighborhood.

00:39:46.210 --> 00:39:50.600
You got these hills where I
think Katy Perry lives up there

00:39:51.682 --> 00:39:55.220
in the hills and then
you go down to the shops

00:39:55.220 --> 00:39:59.130
and you got the, hey
let's go to Little Dom's.

00:39:59.130 --> 00:40:03.623
Let's go to Little Dom's and
get us some Italian food.

00:40:04.959 --> 00:40:06.880
But we stayed in this furnished apartment.

00:40:06.880 --> 00:40:09.640
We knew nothing about where
we should be, it was just--

00:40:09.640 --> 00:40:11.450
It was not decorated,

00:40:11.450 --> 00:40:14.080
it was very cold the way it was decorated,

00:40:14.080 --> 00:40:18.473
like super modern, gray, under-lit.

00:40:19.643 --> 00:40:22.150
It just wasn't how I wanted things to be.

00:40:22.150 --> 00:40:23.130
You like a bright--

00:40:23.130 --> 00:40:25.227
Well I would go in the bathroom
and look in the mirror--

00:40:25.227 --> 00:40:28.260
Antique, bright environment.

00:40:28.260 --> 00:40:30.760
I couldn't see myself
in the freakin' mirror.

00:40:30.760 --> 00:40:33.330
I had to buy a mirror with a...

00:40:34.510 --> 00:40:36.130
I had to buy a vanity mirror,

00:40:36.130 --> 00:40:37.860
like one of those makeup mirrors in order

00:40:37.860 --> 00:40:40.550
to be able to see my
face in my own bathroom.

00:40:40.550 --> 00:40:42.040
It just didn't, you know me,

00:40:42.040 --> 00:40:44.140
I really like things to
be how I like 'em to be.

00:40:44.140 --> 00:40:47.203
Oh really?
So that wasn't home.

00:40:47.203 --> 00:40:48.210
I mean it was a furnished apartment.

00:40:48.210 --> 00:40:50.600
Then we go back and get our
stuff and we come back out,

00:40:50.600 --> 00:40:52.743
we moved into a house, we had a yard,

00:40:55.210 --> 00:40:57.910
but I didn't really know
anybody in the neighborhood

00:40:57.910 --> 00:40:59.900
and a couple of the neighbors that we met,

00:40:59.900 --> 00:41:04.460
it was kinda like, I feel
like you don't like me.

00:41:04.460 --> 00:41:07.740
There was a weird vibe in
the place where we were.

00:41:07.740 --> 00:41:08.573
Yeah.

00:41:10.200 --> 00:41:14.060
And we were, it was from
a financial standpoint,

00:41:14.060 --> 00:41:16.900
things were tenuous for
a number of years there

00:41:16.900 --> 00:41:21.900
as we were moving into
a painful amount of rent

00:41:22.180 --> 00:41:23.013
at these places.

00:41:23.013 --> 00:41:26.630
If you are in the greater Los Angeles area

00:41:26.630 --> 00:41:29.333
with a family,

00:41:30.590 --> 00:41:34.053
you better get it together very quickly.

00:41:35.510 --> 00:41:36.710
Yeah.
Because you will be run

00:41:36.710 --> 00:41:39.810
out of town just because it's so expensive

00:41:39.810 --> 00:41:40.730
to live here, yeah.

00:41:40.730 --> 00:41:42.417
There was definitely a year
or two there it was like

00:41:44.135 --> 00:41:46.480
I don't know if this is sustainable.

00:41:46.480 --> 00:41:50.990
The furnished apartment
that we rented cost

00:41:50.990 --> 00:41:55.623
over three times my
mortgage back in Fuquay.

00:41:57.670 --> 00:42:02.670
It cost over, it cost
seven times my mortgage.

00:42:03.660 --> 00:42:05.310
I think you're just bad at math, dude.

00:42:05.310 --> 00:42:08.147
No, I had bought my house much later

00:42:08.147 --> 00:42:10.750
and my house back home
was pricier than yours.

00:42:10.750 --> 00:42:15.750
Yeah, I got a really good deal on my house

00:42:16.860 --> 00:42:21.140
but yeah, the rent for an
apartment that was smaller

00:42:21.140 --> 00:42:22.580
than the house that I owned.

00:42:22.580 --> 00:42:23.550
But it was furnished.

00:42:23.550 --> 00:42:27.030
Seven times my mortgage.
Yeah.

00:42:27.030 --> 00:42:28.523
That's painful.

00:42:31.590 --> 00:42:33.470
We were fortunate enough to purchase homes

00:42:33.470 --> 00:42:34.950
and I think psychologically,

00:42:34.950 --> 00:42:37.250
you're really putting your
roots down when you're saying

00:42:37.250 --> 00:42:38.913
I'm buying this place.

00:42:40.512 --> 00:42:41.630
It's interesting--
So that's where

00:42:41.630 --> 00:42:42.463
we're at now.

00:42:43.490 --> 00:42:48.480
And I think that you,
with your personality,

00:42:48.480 --> 00:42:51.810
getting everything the
way that you want it

00:42:51.810 --> 00:42:55.620
is a big part of your
personality, less of mine,

00:42:55.620 --> 00:42:57.880
so when I thought about this question,

00:42:57.880 --> 00:43:00.300
I didn't think at all about--

00:43:00.300 --> 00:43:01.830
Physical home.
Physical homes

00:43:01.830 --> 00:43:04.330
and where we're at and being
and where we were before

00:43:04.330 --> 00:43:06.000
and where we're at now.

00:43:06.000 --> 00:43:09.330
It was much more just the vibe that I get

00:43:09.330 --> 00:43:12.373
from the city, you know what I'm saying?

00:43:13.510 --> 00:43:18.510
And I do remember a very specific point

00:43:19.110 --> 00:43:22.280
and that actually was pretty early on.

00:43:22.280 --> 00:43:24.170
It definitely wasn't while
we were in Los Feliz.

00:43:24.170 --> 00:43:25.263
It was after that.

00:43:26.820 --> 00:43:31.820
And where I'd flown back home,
and we still say flying home

00:43:32.360 --> 00:43:33.980
to North Carolina, that's what we say.

00:43:33.980 --> 00:43:36.650
We'll probably always say that regardless

00:43:36.650 --> 00:43:37.800
of how long we're here.

00:43:38.900 --> 00:43:43.083
But I remember arriving back at LAX which,

00:43:43.083 --> 00:43:47.650
it's not the most, it's not
the most welcoming airport.

00:43:47.650 --> 00:43:50.900
When you get to RDU, you're
like do people actually fly

00:43:50.900 --> 00:43:52.330
out of here?

00:43:52.330 --> 00:43:54.750
Yeah.
Is it shut down?

00:43:54.750 --> 00:43:57.320
Because there's not
literally a crowd of people

00:43:57.320 --> 00:43:58.631
that you have to work your way through

00:43:58.631 --> 00:44:01.927
and a million people honking their horns

00:44:01.927 --> 00:44:04.570
as soon as you get to where
you're gonna get picked up.

00:44:04.570 --> 00:44:07.077
But anyway, every time you get to LAX,

00:44:07.077 --> 00:44:08.240
regardless of what time of
say, regardless of what day

00:44:08.240 --> 00:44:10.410
of the year.
It's a circus.

00:44:10.410 --> 00:44:13.840
It's absolutely bonkers
how much traffic there is.

00:44:13.840 --> 00:44:18.840
But I remember getting
back to LAX, walking out

00:44:19.100 --> 00:44:23.920
and waiting for a ride
home and feeling like...

00:44:25.620 --> 00:44:28.830
And I wasn't trying to
feel this, it just hit me,

00:44:28.830 --> 00:44:31.720
I was like, I'm home.

00:44:31.720 --> 00:44:33.980
This feels like home because--

00:44:33.980 --> 00:44:35.420
But can you link it to something?

00:44:35.420 --> 00:44:39.827
I feel like I am now
returning to basically,

00:44:46.820 --> 00:44:48.940
the momentum has shifted.

00:44:48.940 --> 00:44:52.340
There is a, what do you call
it when you get enough--

00:44:52.340 --> 00:44:53.970
Tipping point.
Tipping point,

00:44:53.970 --> 00:44:55.780
but you're like there's enough momentum,

00:44:55.780 --> 00:44:57.930
what is that word that we're looking for?

00:44:57.930 --> 00:44:59.370
Inertia.

00:44:59.370 --> 00:45:00.660
Yeah, we're not saying the word,

00:45:00.660 --> 00:45:02.290
I don't know why I can't
remember it at this point,

00:45:02.290 --> 00:45:04.210
but basically critical mass.

00:45:04.210 --> 00:45:07.460
There's enough critical
mass in Los Angeles now

00:45:07.460 --> 00:45:10.119
and I think it does have
to do with familiarity

00:45:10.119 --> 00:45:12.810
and comfort and so that does have to do

00:45:12.810 --> 00:45:14.900
with the physical home or whatever,

00:45:14.900 --> 00:45:17.347
but it just felt like the critical mass

00:45:17.347 --> 00:45:21.100
of where I kinda considered
my home to be had moved,

00:45:21.100 --> 00:45:25.110
had come with me on the
plane back to Los Angeles

00:45:25.110 --> 00:45:27.290
and now I was stepping
into this environment

00:45:27.290 --> 00:45:31.987
that I felt like I knew,
understood and could thrive in.

00:45:31.987 --> 00:45:33.290
Do you know what I'm saying?

00:45:33.290 --> 00:45:36.110
I know a couple of specific
factors at least for me

00:45:36.110 --> 00:45:37.723
so try these on for size.

00:45:38.575 --> 00:45:41.640
In general sense of community,
actually having friends,

00:45:41.640 --> 00:45:44.640
people that if something goes wrong,

00:45:44.640 --> 00:45:47.520
your impulse is to call
somebody who also lives

00:45:47.520 --> 00:45:50.330
in Los Angeles to get help or advice

00:45:50.330 --> 00:45:54.780
as opposed to calling someone
where you used to live.

00:45:54.780 --> 00:45:57.130
I think that's a telltale sign.

00:45:57.130 --> 00:45:59.190
Who do you call in need?

00:45:59.190 --> 00:46:01.200
And then do you have friends?

00:46:01.200 --> 00:46:02.710
Are you making connections?

00:46:02.710 --> 00:46:07.710
Are your kids and partner
doing the same thing?

00:46:07.870 --> 00:46:12.870
I think also it's like at
the moment you feel hungry

00:46:13.330 --> 00:46:15.480
and you're sittin' on your couch

00:46:15.480 --> 00:46:18.980
and places come to mind
that you can just get

00:46:18.980 --> 00:46:21.036
in the car and drive to.
That are not Bojangles'?

00:46:21.036 --> 00:46:22.180
Yeah.
Right.

00:46:22.180 --> 00:46:23.840
You start thinking of, you know what,

00:46:23.840 --> 00:46:25.550
I'm gonna go and get a so and so.

00:46:25.550 --> 00:46:28.489
I'm gonna get a sub from this place.

00:46:28.489 --> 00:46:32.250
I'm gonna get some Mediterranean
food from this place,

00:46:32.250 --> 00:46:36.270
not the eight other places,
'cause I've tried 'em all.

00:46:36.270 --> 00:46:38.360
I've got my favorites.

00:46:38.360 --> 00:46:40.730
I know where I'm gonna
get the cheeseburger

00:46:40.730 --> 00:46:45.270
and I'm gonna go to the pizza
place and when I go in there,

00:46:45.270 --> 00:46:48.060
the guy's gonna know me by name.

00:46:48.060 --> 00:46:52.510
I mean that's really home
when the pizza guy knows you.

00:46:52.510 --> 00:46:55.440
I think another point is--
We went to the pizza place,

00:46:55.440 --> 00:46:58.647
they were closing and
he said, and we're like,

00:46:58.647 --> 00:47:00.247
"I'm sorry, you're closing."

00:47:01.230 --> 00:47:04.437
And then he was like, "You
know what, place your order.

00:47:04.437 --> 00:47:06.890
"I'll deliver it to your
house," and he knew,

00:47:06.890 --> 00:47:08.900
he knows where our house is.

00:47:08.900 --> 00:47:10.410
Yeah that's a good sign.

00:47:10.410 --> 00:47:11.667
I'm home, baby,

00:47:11.667 --> 00:47:16.667
when I can almost telepathically
order pizza, I'm home.

00:47:17.780 --> 00:47:20.090
And trust me, I think that's probably

00:47:20.090 --> 00:47:25.090
Christy's first measure of
home is do I know my pizza guy.

00:47:26.350 --> 00:47:27.502
I think being able to navigate--

00:47:27.502 --> 00:47:29.110
That probably took six years for her.

00:47:29.110 --> 00:47:33.610
To being able to navigate the landscape

00:47:33.610 --> 00:47:35.530
and not need--
GPS.

00:47:35.530 --> 00:47:39.840
GPS, I think is, now,
if you live in any city,

00:47:39.840 --> 00:47:42.131
you probably always have your GPS on

00:47:42.131 --> 00:47:44.186
because you never know when
you're gonna run into--

00:47:44.186 --> 00:47:47.233
Variables.
Traffic and accidents

00:47:47.233 --> 00:47:48.500
and stuff like that but--

00:47:48.500 --> 00:47:51.290
But you won't get lost if your phone dies.

00:47:51.290 --> 00:47:55.784
Yeah 'cause LA is huge and I remember,

00:47:55.784 --> 00:47:57.563
we used to come out here,

00:47:58.670 --> 00:48:01.460
before we were doing Commercial
Kings we would come out here

00:48:01.460 --> 00:48:04.900
'cause of course we did that
amazing hit show on the CW

00:48:04.900 --> 00:48:07.103
in 2007 called Online Nation.

00:48:08.030 --> 00:48:10.583
So good it got canceled
after four episodes.

00:48:12.130 --> 00:48:13.930
But we would come out to work on that

00:48:13.930 --> 00:48:15.520
and then to come out
and pitch other things

00:48:15.520 --> 00:48:18.010
and we were trying to
make things happen here

00:48:18.010 --> 00:48:22.990
and we would stay at the
Beverly Garland Hotel on Vine.

00:48:24.950 --> 00:48:25.783
What is that on?

00:48:27.970 --> 00:48:28.990
Vineland.
Vineland.

00:48:28.990 --> 00:48:33.770
I just passed it the other
night where you're on Ventura

00:48:33.770 --> 00:48:36.490
'cause we went to eat with
some people in Studio City.

00:48:36.490 --> 00:48:39.910
We were on Ventura,
took a left on Vineland

00:48:39.910 --> 00:48:41.810
and you go past that
little shopping center

00:48:41.810 --> 00:48:45.369
that had the City Walk that
we would walk to the City Walk

00:48:45.369 --> 00:48:47.783
down from the Beverly Garland.
Yeah.

00:48:48.860 --> 00:48:51.120
Our world was--
That block.

00:48:51.120 --> 00:48:53.211
That block and then if we were to drive,

00:48:53.211 --> 00:48:56.960
we'd take the 101 through the mountains

00:48:56.960 --> 00:48:59.390
down to somewhere in
Hollywood to do something,

00:48:59.390 --> 00:49:01.049
to have a meeting, maybe go
all the way to Beverly Hills.

00:49:01.049 --> 00:49:01.882
Yeah that's all we knew.

00:49:01.882 --> 00:49:05.840
But that was it and that's
just a very small sliver

00:49:05.840 --> 00:49:09.420
of Los Angeles and then
once you can kinda like,

00:49:09.420 --> 00:49:13.150
oh I can draw, for me, I know this is,

00:49:13.150 --> 00:49:16.840
Jessie couldn't do this
because I annoy her

00:49:16.840 --> 00:49:21.000
to the point that I try to
understand the geography

00:49:21.000 --> 00:49:22.683
in my mind.
Yeah.

00:49:23.750 --> 00:49:25.840
But for me that was a big point.

00:49:25.840 --> 00:49:28.760
'Cause in North Carolina, of course,

00:49:28.760 --> 00:49:31.650
when we were in North Carolina,
at least up until the end,

00:49:31.650 --> 00:49:33.641
there was no GPS.

00:49:33.641 --> 00:49:36.240
You didn't use GPS to get anywhere.

00:49:36.240 --> 00:49:38.460
You could get everywhere you needed to go

00:49:38.460 --> 00:49:40.740
by just, you just knew the environment

00:49:40.740 --> 00:49:43.010
and you kinda always knew where you were.

00:49:43.010 --> 00:49:45.770
So when that happened
to me in Los Angeles,

00:49:45.770 --> 00:49:46.960
it started to feel like home.

00:49:46.960 --> 00:49:49.390
That was a big moment.

00:49:49.390 --> 00:49:53.320
And Britton's been living
with us for half a year.

00:49:53.320 --> 00:49:56.520
He's traveling a lot
with the opportunities

00:49:56.520 --> 00:49:59.550
that he has for making music.

00:49:59.550 --> 00:50:02.630
But our home is his home base but...

00:50:06.526 --> 00:50:08.290
I don't think I'm going
out on a limb here to say

00:50:08.290 --> 00:50:10.420
that he doesn't consider Los Angeles home.

00:50:10.420 --> 00:50:11.253
After six months--

